pancake treats performance as a public contract, but it enforces that contract two
different ways, and the difference is the whole point of this page. Where a cost can be
pinned to an exact number, a build gate holds the number. Where it cannot, because wall-clock
throughput moves with your CPU, your build mode, and your traffic, pancake reports the
figure instead of asserting it, and never lets an adjective stand in for a measurement.
So there are no "N times faster" claims anywhere else on this site; the place a speed
number is allowed to live is a benchmark you can run, and that is this page.

## What the build gates

These are not benchmarks. They are assertions that fail `zig build check` if they drift.

- **One allocation per delivery.** A steady-state `op:deliver` to a live export costs
  exactly one heap allocation: the owned-frame copy the event model requires. The
  alloc-budget test gates that with a `== 1` assertion, so a stray allocation on the hot
  path breaks the build instead of slipping into a release.
- **Per-connection footprint.** A footprint budget test holds the protocol state one
  idle connection keeps under a low, fixed ceiling (about two kilobytes), because a
  single node is meant to carry on the order of half a million live sessions and memory
  is the binding constraint. The
  [ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md) owns
  the exact ceiling and the arithmetic behind it.

## What the benchmarks report

These print numbers; they do not gate them. No test asserts a wall-clock bound, because
an honest one needs a stable, release-pinned harness that does not exist yet.

| Benchmark | Measures | Run it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| codec | encode and decode ns/op and MB/s for both codecs, plus steady-state dispatch | `zig build bench-codec` |
| peer scaling | the marginal heap one more connection costs, extrapolated to 500k | `zig build bench-peer-scaling` |
| frame view | the owned-`Frame` to borrowed-`FrameView` path | `zig build bench-frame-view` |

`bench-peer-scaling` writes a report under `zig-out/bench-reports/` with the
per-connection footprint and how many connections fit in common RAM sizes.

## How to read the numbers

Two cautions, both straight from the harnesses:

- **Build mode dominates.** Under `zig build check` the benchmarks run in Debug, where
  throughput is roughly an order of magnitude below `ReleaseFast`. Read the printed
  `mode=` field. Debug numbers are for catching a regression against the previous Debug
  run; for absolute figures, run with `-Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.
- **Footprint is core state, not socket RSS.** The peer-scaling number is the sans-io
  core's heap, which is the dominant per-connection cost once read buffers come from a
  shared pool rather than per-connection. Real socket-level RSS and syscall rate under a
  live 500k load still need a measurement pancake has not run, and it says so plainly.

## The codec trade, specifically

[Syrup]($link.page('codecs')) packs a message into compact, variable-length byte
records; Cap'n Proto lays the same message out as larger, fixed-width words with every
field at a known offset. Which one wins on encode and decode, and by how much, is
exactly what `bench-codec` exists to answer, so this page will not put a multiplier on
it. Run the benchmark on the hardware you care about.

## A measured look: the same app on pancake and Goblins

*Run on 2026-06-12, in the project devcontainer: aarch64 Linux (linuxkit 6.12), 16 logical
CPUs, 8 GiB RAM, Racket v9.2 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0, and `libpancake` built by Zig
0.16.0.*

pancake's Racket binding and [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) both
speak OCapN, so one Racket application runs unchanged on either and can be timed head to
head. That is all `zig build bench-goblins` does: one application source per substrate,
four workloads — an echo, a capability registry, a mint and purse, and a task board —
driven identically with synchronous round-trips and no promise pipelining on either side,
over real loopback TCP with `TCP_NODELAY` set on both ends. Each run warms the path, forces
a collection, and samples the live set, so the only thing that differs between the two
columns is the substrate underneath. The
[suite README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bench/goblins/README.md)
writes that fairness model down in full.

These are one person's numbers, measured this way, on the machine named above. The honest
move is to measure your own: `zig build bench-goblins` — it needs `racket` and the `goblins`
collection on `PATH`; append `-- --smoke` for a fast pass. Per-connection memory is in the
harness output but left off here, since at this scale it is swamped by allocator and GC
accounting.

| suite | Goblins p50 (ms) | pancake p50 (ms) | Goblins req/s | pancake req/s |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| echo | 0.36 | 0.06 | 2077 | 15117 |
| registry | 0.66 | 0.12 | 1176 | 8118 |
| mint | 0.73 | 0.11 | 1145 | 8205 |
| taskboard | 1.35 | 0.23 | 614 | 2430 |

